<PageLayout
  title="What a melanocortin receptor actually looks like — and how it flips on"
  kicker="First light"
  description="For decades the melanocortin receptors were known only by what they did. Between 2020 and 2023, cryo-EM and crystallography resolved all five at atomic detail — the seven-helix fold, the calcium hidden in the pocket, and the outward swing of TM6 that switches the system on."
  thread="A system this old wrote its instructions in shape long before we could read them; the structures are us learning, at last, to see the letters."
  updated="2026"
  about={[
    { name: 'Melanocortin receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_receptor'] },
    { name: 'Melanocortin 4 receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_4_receptor'] },
    { name: 'G protein-coupled receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G_protein-coupled_receptor'] },
    { name: 'Cryogenic electron microscopy', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cryogenic_electron_microscopy'] },
  ]}
>
  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'For most of their history the melanocortin receptors were known only by what they did. Between 2020 and 2023, cryo-EM and crystallography resolved all five — MC1R through MC5R — at atomic detail.',
      'All five share one fold: seven helices around a shallow pocket. That family resemblance is exactly why a drug aimed at one receptor so easily touches another.',
      'The first MC4R structure held a surprise — a calcium ion sitting inside the binding pocket, needed for high-affinity agonist binding. Calcium is a genuine cofactor of the melanocortin signal.',
      'Switching on is a mechanical move: the ligand’s His-Phe-Arg-Trp core settles in the pocket, and transmembrane helix 6 swings outward to open the receptor’s inner face for the Gs protein.',
      'The structures now explain the drugs — why setmelanotide activates MC4R while the near-identical SHU9119 blocks it, and why building an MC4R drug that spares MC1R (the tan) is so hard.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    For almost forty years we knew the melanocortin receptors the way you know
    someone by their reputation and never their face. We knew MC1R decided how
    dark skin ran; that MC4R sat at the centre of appetite; that MC2R answered
    the stress axis. We could measure what bound them and what they did. We had
    never actually seen one. Then, in a rush that ran from 2020 to 2023, the
    whole family sat for its portrait.<Cite n={1} /> This is what the pictures show.
  </p>

  <h2>The shape: seven helices and a shallow pocket</h2>
  <p>
    Every melanocortin receptor belongs to the largest and best-studied class of
    signalling proteins, the <a href="/glossary">G-protein-coupled receptors</a>.
    The shared design is old and economical: seven helices threaded back and
    forth through the cell membrane, packed into a bundle. The outward-facing end
    forms a shallow pocket where the signal binds; the inward-facing end, on the
    cytoplasmic side, is where the receptor hands its message to a G protein. What
    crosses the membrane is never the drug and never the hormone – only a change
    in shape, and then a second messenger.
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="A melanocortin receptor is a seven-helix bundle in the membrane, its pocket facing outward and its G-protein coupling site facing in. TM6 is picked out because it is the helix that moves when the receptor switches on."
    source="Schematic topology, not an atomic structure. Fold as resolved for MC4R (Yu 2020; Israeli 2021) and MC1R (Ma 2021)."
  >
    <ReceptorFold />
  </Figure>

  <p>
    Melanocortin receptors are among the <em>smallest</em> of these proteins –
    short outer tails, a compact pocket – which is part of why they proved hard
    to crystallise for so long, and part of why the whole family looks so alike.
    All five, MC1R through MC5R, are built on the one fold and grip their ligands
    the same way.<Cite n={3} /> That resemblance is not a detail. It is the origin
    of nearly every side effect in the field: a molecule shaped to fit one of
    these pockets will very nearly fit the other four.
  </p>

  <h2>First light: the cryo-EM wave</h2>
  <p>
    The breakthrough came from <a href="/glossary">cryo-electron microscopy</a>{' '}
    and a last round of crystallography arriving together. MC4R went first, in
    2020 – a crystal structure caught with the blocker SHU9119 in its
    pocket.<Cite n={1} /> Within a year the field had the receptor switched
    <em>on</em>: an active MC4R captured mid-signal, bound to setmelanotide and
    coupled to its G protein.<Cite n={2} /> MC1R, the pigment receptor, was
    resolved the same year with α-MSH and with afamelanotide in place;<Cite n={3} />
    a second group solved four MC4R structures at once, including one holding a
    non-peptide drug;<Cite n={4} /> a third mapped the active receptor at high
    resolution.<Cite n={5} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The stragglers followed in 2023. MC2R – the odd receptor that answers only to{' '}
    <a href="/acth">ACTH</a> – was solved together with the small partner protein
    it cannot work without;<Cite n={7} /> MC3R and MC5R, the two least-studied of
    the family, were resolved in the same paper.<Cite n={6} /> By the end of that
    year every one of the five had been seen. Since then the work has kept
    going: an engineered <a href="/glossary">nanobody</a> built to switch MC4R on
    by itself,<Cite n={9} /> and a 2025 set of the receptors with nothing bound at
    all, explaining why they hum with a little activity even when empty.<Cite n={8} />
  </p>

  <h2>The calcium in the pocket</h2>
  <p>
    The first structure held a genuine surprise. Sitting inside the MC4R binding
    pocket, alongside the ligand, was a <strong>calcium ion</strong> – held in
    place by a small set of acidic receptor residues and reaching out to touch
    the ligand itself.<Cite n={1} /> This was not incidental. Calcium sharply
    raises how tightly agonists bind: take it away and agonist affinity falls by
    something like ten- to a hundred-fold. The ion is not packing material. It is
    part of the recognition.
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="The ligand’s His-Phe-Arg-Trp core descends into the pocket; a calcium ion sits between it and acidic residues on the wall, coordinating both. Agonists need the calcium to bind tightly; antagonists that occupy the same pocket do not."
    source="After Yu 2020 (MC4R, PDB-resolved Ca²⁺ site) and Israeli 2021; the shared calcium site is also seen in MC1R (Ma 2021) and other MC4R structures (Heyder 2021; Zhang 2021)."
  >
    <CalciumPocket />
  </Figure>

  <p>
    The same calcium site turned up in structure after structure – MC1R, the
    other MC4R complexes – which told the field it was a family trait, not a
    quirk of one receptor.<Cite n={3} /><Cite n={5} /> And it drew a sharp line
    between the two kinds of ligand. Agonists, the molecules that switch the
    receptor on, lean on the calcium. Antagonists occupy the very same pocket but
    do not need it.<Cite n={2} /> The distinction the whole system runs on –
    on versus off – is written partly in whether a single ion is invited to help.
  </p>

  <h2>How the switch flips</h2>
  <p>
    Every melanocortin peptide shares a four-residue core – His-Phe-Arg-Trp, the{' '}
    <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a> signature – and the structures show it doing
    exactly what decades of pharmacology predicted: settling into the pocket, with
    the calcium bridging it to the receptor wall. What happens next is
    mechanical. On the far side of the membrane, transmembrane helix 6 swings
    outward at its cytoplasmic end, opening a cavity on the receptor’s inner face.
    Into that opening the <strong>Gs protein</strong> docks, and the familiar
    signal – cyclic AMP – begins.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="Left: with no agonist, TM6 is packed in and the inner cavity is shut. Right: the agonist binds, TM6 swings out, the cavity opens, and Gs couples to make cAMP. An antagonist fills the same pocket but jams the hinge, so the helix cannot move."
    source="After Israeli 2021 (active MC4R–Gs; the TM6 outward movement and the SHU9119 comparison) and Zhang 2021."
  >
    <ActivationSwitch />
  </Figure>

  <p>
    It is worth being honest about the limits of the cartoon. The melanocortin
    receptors are unusual class-A GPCRs and break several of the tidy rules other
    receptors follow, so the exact set of internal “microswitches” is better read
    from the papers than recited as a fixed list.<Cite n={4} /> But the headline is
    solid and repeated across every structure: agonist in the pocket, TM6 out,
    G protein in.
  </p>

  <h2>Running it backwards</h2>
  <p>
    If activation is TM6 swinging out, blocking it is keeping TM6 in. The classic
    research antagonist <a href="/shu9119">SHU9119</a> binds the same pocket as an
    agonist, but a single bulky, unnatural side chain wedges against the hinge and
    stops the helix moving – the molecule occupies the switch without throwing
    it.<Cite n={2} /> There is a beautiful complication here: the very same
    SHU9119 that <em>blocks</em> MC4R actually <em>activates</em> MC1R, and it was
    caught doing so in the MC1R structure.<Cite n={3} /> A ligand is not simply an
    agonist or an antagonist. It is one or the other depending on which of the
    five pockets it finds itself in.
  </p>
  <p>
    The body has its own blockers. <a href="/agrp">AgRP</a> and the pigment brake{' '}
    <a href="/asip">agouti (ASIP)</a> are not merely competitors; they are
    <strong>inverse agonists</strong>, pushing the receptors below their resting
    idle rather than just keeping the natural signal out.<Cite n={10} /> That
    resting idle is real – the 2025 empty-receptor structures were solved
    precisely to explain the family’s built-in background activity, the thing an
    inverse agonist turns down.<Cite n={8} /> One honest gap remains: no one has yet
    captured an AgRP- or agouti-bound receptor directly, so how these brakes sit
    in the pocket is still read from function and modelling, not from a picture.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond cAMP: what “biased” means</h2>
  <p>
    A receptor is not a simple on/off lamp with one wire. MC4R can talk through Gs
    and cyclic AMP, through a different G protein called Gq/11, and – strangest of
    all – straight to an ion channel. A <strong>biased</strong> agonist is one
    that leans on some of these routes more than others, because the particular
    shape it locks the receptor into favours one exit over another. The approved
    drug setmelanotide, for one, is described as biased toward the Gq/11
    route.<Cite n={5} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The strangest route skips G proteins entirely. In hypothalamic neurons, MC4R
    can open and close the potassium channel <strong>Kir7.1</strong> directly:
    α-MSH closes it and AgRP opens it, changing whether the neuron fires –
    without a G protein in between.<Cite n={11} /> The finding was unusual enough
    to be argued over for years; it now has support from several groups and, as of
    2026, a structure of Kir7.1 with MC4R to give the mechanism a shape.<Cite n={12} />
    It is a good reminder that these receptors are still handing us surprises.
  </p>

  <h2>Why MC2R needs a chaperone</h2>
  <p>
    One receptor refused to behave like the others. <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>,
    the adrenal receptor for ACTH, cannot even reach the cell surface on its own,
    let alone bind its hormone. It needs a small partner protein, MRAP1, and the
    2023 structure showed why: MRAP1 wraps across the receptor like a seat-belt,
    reshaping the entrance so that ACTH – much larger than the little
    His-Phe-Arg-Trp core – can be read.<Cite n={7} /> It is the clearest case in
    the family of a receptor that is only half a machine by itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    A close cousin of that helper, MRAP2, tunes MC4R and MC3R instead of MC2R,
    shifting how strongly they signal and how they pair up.<Cite n={13} /> That
    complex has not yet been solved atomically; for now it is known from function.
    The distinction is worth keeping straight, because the two are easy to
    confuse: MRAP1 is MC2R’s indispensable partner, MRAP2 is the modulator of the
    appetite receptors.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the pictures matter</h2>
  <p>
    Structures are not decoration; they change what can be built. They already
    explain the drugs we have – why setmelanotide switches MC4R on while the
    almost-identical SHU9119 does not comes straight out of comparing the two in
    the same pocket,<Cite n={2} /><Cite n={5} /> and why a small non-peptide
    molecule can be made to fit at all was answered by catching one in the
    act.<Cite n={4} /> They also explain the field’s central frustration. The
    shared pocket and the shared calcium trick, so elegant across MC1R–MC5R, are
    exactly why an MC4R appetite drug spills onto MC1R and darkens the skin, or
    brushes the adrenal MC2R.<Cite n={6} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The encouraging part is that the pictures are starting to solve the problem
    they revealed. Structure-guided redesign of setmelanotide’s core has already
    produced a compound with more than a hundred-fold preference for MC4R over
    MC1R – an early, deliberate attempt to keep the appetite effect and leave the
    pigment alone.<Cite n={14} /> And wholly new kinds of key are being cut: an
    engineered nanobody that switches MC4R on by itself, a tool that did not exist
    before the structures made it thinkable.<Cite n={9} /> A system we could only
    describe five years ago is one we are beginning to design against.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose mt-8 rounded-lg border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--accent-soft)] p-6 text-sm leading-relaxed">
    <p class="font-medium text-[var(--text)]">How we know this</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[var(--text)]">
      The structures on this page come from cryo-electron microscopy and X-ray
      crystallography, published in the peer-reviewed literature and deposited in
      public structure databases. This is young science – most of it has arrived
      since 2020 – and a few things named here, such as an AgRP-bound receptor or
      an MRAP2–MC4R complex, have not yet been directly visualised; where a claim
      rests on function or modelling rather than a solved structure, the text says
      so. The figures are schematic teaching diagrams, not renderings of specific
      atomic coordinates. See the <a href="/about">editorial standards</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

